{
 "id": "2026-10-18-jurassic-flightless-bird",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "This early bird could not fly. So what was it built for?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "In a study published in Nature, Chinese researchers describe Yuezhuornis. It lived about one hundred fifty million years ago, in what is now Fujian, China. The fossil was found in twenty twenty four. Its body mass is estimated at about eight hundred twenty grams."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "Its forelimbs were very short, which the authors call strong evidence of a life on the ground. Its limb proportions match living ground birds, such as pheasants. But it kept dinosaur traits: serrated teeth, and a flexible second toe with a sickle-shaped claw."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Lead author Wang Min says this challenges the idea that early birds evolved mainly for flight. The caveat: it is one incomplete specimen. Its lifestyle and exact place on the family tree need more fossils."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will more flightless Jurassic birds turn up? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
